## 9. How to make race conditions easier to find

If you are trying to improve detection, do the opposite of evasion:

### Use clear names

Bad:

```java
private boolean f;
private String s;
```

Better:

```java
private boolean sessionReady;
private String sessionMessage;
```

### Add comments where concurrency is non-obvious

```java
// This field is shared across threads and must be updated atomically.
private final AtomicReference<Session> currentSession = new AtomicReference<>();
```

### Write concurrency tests

Example:

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.RepeatedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class SafeStatusTest {
    @RepeatedTest(50)
    public void readShouldNeverSeeStaleState() throws Exception {
        SafeStatus status = new SafeStatus();
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);

        for (int i = 0; i < 1_000; i++) {
            pool.submit(() -> status.publish("hello"));
            pool.submit(() -> {
                String value = status.read();
                if (value != null && !value.equals("hello")) {
                    throw new AssertionError("Unexpected value: " + value);
                }
            });
        }

        pool.shutdown();
        pool.awaitTermination(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

### Use repeated tests

Race conditions are probabilistic, so a single test run may pass even when the code is broken.

Use:

```java
@RepeatedTest(100)
```

or run the test suite many times in CI.

